      "id": 1,
      "text": "Mooresville was named after early settlers Robert and William Moore who established homesteads on the land that became the town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mooresville%2C_Alabama",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Early 19th‑century homes, including one that once housed Andrew Johnson’s tailor apprenticeship, line Mooresville’s streets.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://mooresvilleal.com/history-of-mooresville/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The entire town, described as a picturesque early 19th‑century village, is listed on the National Register of Historic Places.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mooresville%2C_Alabama",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Mooresville served as the primary filming location for Disney’s 1995 live‑action movie Tom and Huck.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mooresville%2C_Alabama",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Mooresville once thrived as a cotton‑farming hub until the early 20th‑century boll weevil devastated the industry.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mooresville%2C_Alabama",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Mooresville became one of Alabama’s oldest incorporated towns on November 16, 1818, while the state was still a territory.",
